      "text": "Given the equations of the two lines:\n\n\\[\ny = mx + 4\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 3x + b\n\\]\n\nWe know that these lines intersect at the point \\((6, 10)\\). Therefore, the coordinates \\((6, 10)\\) must satisfy both equations. We can start by substituting \\(x = 6\\) and \\(y = 10\\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[\n10 = m \\cdot 6 + 4\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(m\\):\n\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4\n\\]\n\\[\n10 - 4 = 6m\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = 6m\n\\]\n\\[\nm = 1\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = 6\\) and \\(y = 10\\) into the second equation to verify and find \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n10 = 3 \\cdot 6 + b\n\\]\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b\n\\]\n\\[\n10 - 18 = b\n\\]\n\\[\nb = -8\n\\]\n\nNow we have the values of \\(m\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nm = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = -8\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(b + m\\):\n\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
